A World of Warcraft server emulator is one of the better distributed-systems
textbooks lying around in public, and most of it is not distributed at all.

These projects exist to study game server architecture and network protocol
design, and that is the only use I am discussing. Running one against live
retail services, or as a commercial service, is neither endorsed nor covered
here — if you run anything, its legality is yours to answer for.

Split the system honestly and almost all of it is boring stateless plumbing
that belongs in a cluster. Exactly one process does not, and it is the one that
is the game.

::: note
**Part 6 of 7 of [Where to start with self-hosting](/blog/running-things-on-your-own-hardware/)**, a series about self-hosting a platform, and about whether you should.

Previous: [Docker Swarm instead of a k3s platform](/blog/docker-swarm-instead/)

Next: [A software factory on your own cluster](/blog/software-factory-on-your-own-cluster/)
